591	// Browser-chrome compensation.
592	//
593	// Even in headless mode, Chrome/Edge reserves pixels for the title bar,
594	// tab strip, omnibox, and scrollbar gutter, so a `--window-size=W,H`
595	// produces an INNER viewport of ~(W-24) × (H-92). Probed on Edge 147
596	// (both legacy `--headless` and `--headless=new`) — and the same
597	// subtraction has been present since Chrome 60+ on Windows.
598	//
599	// Result (without compensation): a 900×328 window renders the first
600	// 876×236 pixels of content, and the remaining 64w × 92h area of the
601	// screenshot is painted with body background (white). Users reported it
602	// as "image cut off halfway with blank below".
603	//
604	// Fix: pad the window by (W_SLACK, H_SLACK) — both generous (~1.5× the
605	// observed miss) to absorb version drift — then crop the resulting PNG
606	// back to exactly the requested W × H. The chrome-reserved area is
607	// outside the crop box, so users see a pixel-perfect target-size PNG.
608	// ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
609	const CHROME_W_SLACK = 40;
610	const CHROME_H_SLACK = 140;
